WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.906
Pointing your own domain at your own PageMotor server.

00:00:03.906 --> 00:00:11.413
The records to add, the stale ones to delete, and the order that keeps Let's Encrypt happy when Cloudflare is in front.

00:00:12.813 --> 00:00:13.529
Step one.

00:00:13.529 --> 00:00:23.155
Ask the server for its own address, rather than trusting a hosting panel that might show you an internal or floating one.

00:00:23.155 --> 00:00:24.905
curl -4 icanhazip.com.

00:00:24.905 --> 00:00:29.439
Whatever it prints is the address every record points at.

00:00:30.839 --> 00:00:35.675
Step two. In the DNS panel for the domain, add two A records.

00:00:35.675 --> 00:00:39.798
One named @, for the bare domain, and one named www.

00:00:39.798 --> 00:00:41.939
Both point at that address.

00:00:41.939 --> 00:00:56.368
If A records already exist, edit them rather than adding a second, because two A records with different addresses on the same name splits your visitors between two servers at random.

00:00:56.368 --> 00:00:58.192
Leave MX and TXT alone.

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:03.006
Step three is the one that catches people.

00:01:03.006 --> 00:01:10.567
Look for AAAA records on @ and www, and delete them unless your server genuinely serves IPv6.

00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:24.957
A browser that sees both an AAAA and an A record usually tries the IPv6 address first, so a stale one sends visitors to the old host while your own tests over IPv4 look perfect.

00:01:24.957 --> 00:01:33.168
Let's Encrypt prefers IPv6 too, so a stale AAAA can fail issuance even when your A record is correct.

00:01:34.568 --> 00:01:38.301
Step four, and only if Cloudflare is in your DNS chain.

00:01:38.301 --> 00:01:43.663
Set both records to the grey cloud, DNS only, before you request a certificate.

00:01:43.663 --> 00:01:53.776
The challenge fetches a file from your domain over plain HTTP, and with the proxy on, that request lands on Cloudflare's edge instead of your server.

00:01:53.776 --> 00:01:56.627
Then issue the certificate for both names.

00:01:58.027 --> 00:01:59.701
Step five is optional.

00:01:59.701 --> 00:02:06.166
Once the certificate is installed you can flip both records back to the orange cloud.

00:02:06.166 --> 00:02:09.818
One setting decides whether that works or loops.

00:02:09.818 --> 00:02:12.480
SSL/TLS mode must be Full (strict).

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:23.130
Flexible makes Cloudflare talk to your server over plain HTTP, your server redirects to HTTPS, and the browser dies with too many redirects.

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:27.238
It is the most reported symptom of this whole process.

00:02:28.638 --> 00:02:31.747
Check it from your own machine, not the server.

00:02:31.747 --> 00:02:36.445
Dig the A records for both names. Dig the AAAA and expect nothing back.

00:02:36.445 --> 00:02:43.127
Then curl the site and look for a clean 200, rather than a certificate error or a chain of redirects.

00:02:44.527 --> 00:02:45.448
Two footnotes.

00:02:45.448 --> 00:02:50.381
A domain bought through Cloudflare Registrar cannot change its nameservers.

00:02:50.381 --> 00:02:56.170
That is by design, and the only route out is a transfer, which ICANN blocks for 60 days.

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:58.998
And a 403 at this stage is usually correct:

00:02:58.998 --> 00:03:04.589
it means the domain reaches your server, and the document root is simply still empty.

00:03:05.989 --> 00:03:10.633
The full guide has every command, and a troubleshooting section for each of those symptoms.
